In that situation, there will be stocks.

183
00:21:49,320 --> 00:21:56,060
If the sector's up 215%, there's going to
be stocks in that sector that are up 120%.

184
00:21:56,060 --> 00:22:00,060
There's going to be stocks in that sector
that are up 340%.

185
00:22:00,060 --> 00:22:05,260
The sector is the average return of all
the stocks in it.

186
00:22:08,300 --> 00:22:09,540
What about utilities?

187
00:22:12,240 --> 00:22:13,640
Significantly underperform the market.

188
00:22:14,320 --> 00:22:18,940
Significantly underperform all the
cyclical sectors we've looked at.

189
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:22,720
So you can see with the price action that
this actually works.

190
00:22:23,380 --> 00:22:30,100
You know, cyclicals really do outperform
by a huge amount defensive sectors when
GDP expands.

191
00:22:31,880 --> 00:22:35,520
And they underperform massively when GDP
contracts.

192
00:22:36,420 --> 00:22:46,000
So you're obviously going to be leaning
towards cyclicals, sectors and stocks when
you believe GDP is going to expand.

193
00:22:46,620 --> 00:22:51,180
And you can see it's not that difficult to
connect the dots.

194
00:22:52,080 --> 00:22:59,260
When you see for the first time the ISM
contraction is slowing for the first time
in over a year.

195
00:23:01,200 --> 00:23:03,040
That's when GDP has bottomed.

196
00:23:04,140 --> 00:23:05,600
GDP contraction has bottomed.

197
00:23:07,200 --> 00:23:10,600
And you should be piling into cyclicals.

198
00:23:10,600 --> 00:23:20,460
And then conversely, when GDP slows,
growth slows for the first time, you need
to be piling into defensives.

199
00:23:23,420 --> 00:23:25,520
And looking to go short cyclicals.

200
00:23:27,600 --> 00:23:32,580
But all based on the premise that you're
going to outperform the sector and the
market.

201
00:23:32,840 --> 00:23:34,040
Otherwise, there's no point.

202
00:23:34,040 --> 00:23:34,080
There's no point.

203
00:23:40,600 --> 00:23:44,260
So I'm sure you all know what beta is, the
market return.

204
00:23:46,080 --> 00:23:49,320
Positive alpha returns over and above
beta.

205
00:23:50,800 --> 00:23:54,980
Negative alpha returns below the market
return, beta.

206
00:23:56,660 --> 00:24:02,200
Your job as a trader, if you're going
through the asset classes, if you trade
all the asset classes,

207
00:24:04,360 --> 00:24:09,740
your job as a trader, if you choose to
take positions in sectors and stocks, is
to outperform the market.

208
00:24:10,600 --> 00:24:13,060
Otherwise, it's dead money.

209
00:24:14,540 --> 00:24:17,140
You might as well have owned the market,
shorted the market.

210
00:24:18,520 --> 00:24:24,300
You might as well have taken a position in
the commodities, currencies or rates.

211
00:24:26,240 --> 00:24:29,060
Because you probably would have got a
higher return for lower risk.

212
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:30,780
And that's the key.

213
00:24:33,340 --> 00:24:37,900
Your job as a trader in the stock market
is not just to generate alpha, positive
alpha.

214
00:24:39,460 --> 00:24:43,460
But it's also to generate that with the
lowest possible risk.

215
00:24:45,740 --> 00:24:48,700
As traders, you want to go where the money
is.

216
00:24:50,160 --> 00:24:54,420
But you also want to go where the money is
and take the least amount of risk possible
to get it.

217
00:24:55,240 --> 00:24:55,940
That's your job.

218
00:24:58,280 --> 00:25:05,400
So I find it quite hilarious when you, for
example, look online or turn the TV on or
read a newspaper

219
00:25:05,400 --> 00:25:14,600
and you see people creating negative
headlines about hedge funds that have
underperformed the market.

220
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:16,520
Totally irrelevant.

221
00:25:20,100 --> 00:25:27,100
Because if that hedge fund manager's
mandate is that they're looking for a 12%
return per year

222
00:25:27,100 --> 00:25:31,640
on 8% volatility, it doesn't matter what
the market does.

223
00:25:32,200 --> 00:25:35,180
Because that's why the investors have
given their money to that hedge fund
manager.

224
00:25:41,060 --> 00:25:49,980
So that hedge fund manager is taking less
risk than the rest of the market to get a
certain return.

225
00:25:55,180 --> 00:26:02,360
So if our job as traders is to get the
highest amount of return possible for the
least amount of risk,

226
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:09,740
there's various structures of trades you
can do to create that situation.

227
00:26:13,180 --> 00:26:22,820
So cross-sector spread trades, where, for
example, you go long the S&P 500 consumer
discretionary sector

228
00:26:22,820 --> 00:26:28,340
and short the utility sector and hedge out
market risk in the process.

229
00:26:29,700 --> 00:26:32,740
So when you hedge out the market risk,
you're taking less risk

230
00:26:32,740 --> 00:26:37,600
to get the returns that the sectors are
going to give you.

231
00:26:39,560 --> 00:26:40,480
Does that make sense?

232
00:26:42,520 --> 00:26:47,000
We'll come on to how you do it, but assume
that you've hedged out one risk

233
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,960
in the list of all the risks that you've
got exposure to, yeah?

234
00:26:52,600 --> 00:26:54,680
Cross-sector constituent spread trades.

235
00:26:55,240 --> 00:26:57,600
So let's go into the consumer
discretionary sector

236
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:02,020
and pick a stock that we think is going to
massively outperform both the sector and
the market.

237
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:10,980
So we look at a restaurant stock in the
US, Chipotle Mexican Grill.

238
00:27:11,860 --> 00:27:20,440
So we think their EPS growth is going to
be ridiculous and it's going to outperform
the rest of the sector.

239
00:27:21,960 --> 00:27:23,600
And the sector is going to outperform the
market.

240
00:27:26,400 --> 00:27:37,080
And we go short, Entergy, which is a
utility in the United States, a big $11
billion stock in the United States.

241
00:27:38,880 --> 00:27:44,660
So they are constituents of the consumer
discretionary sector and the utility
sector.

242
00:27:44,880 --> 00:28:00,120
So you think there's going to be a
consumer discretionary stock that will
underperform its sector and underperform
the market as a whole.

243
00:28:02,360 --> 00:28:08,900
And you use that to hedge out risk in
Chipotle Mexican Grill.

244
00:28:10,440 --> 00:28:13,240
So you're hedging out market risk and
sector risk.

245
00:28:15,440 --> 00:28:16,380
Does that make sense?

246
00:28:17,700 --> 00:28:22,960
So you're trying to get paid the highest
possible return for the least amount of
risk.

247
00:28:27,960 --> 00:28:30,880
Now let's go through an example in the UK.

248
00:28:32,440 --> 00:28:35,300
Just so you can try to understand the
approach.

249
00:28:36,840 --> 00:28:41,720
Let's have a look at Long Tesco, Short
Sainsbury's.

250
00:28:42,840 --> 00:28:48,220
That's a classic spread trade within the
retail space in the UK.

251
00:28:51,420 --> 00:28:53,420
So they're both essentially supermarkets.

252
00:28:55,700 --> 00:29:05,440
Now if you'd have put that spread trade on
in 1987, that trade would have paid you
1,300%.

253
00:29:05,440 --> 00:29:12,840
So Tesco outperformed Sainsbury by 1,300%.

254
00:29:12,840 --> 00:29:16,900
The return in Tesco was 1,400%.

255
00:29:16,900 --> 00:29:19,900
The return in Sainsbury's was 100%.

256
00:29:21,420 --> 00:29:31,400
So essentially, you've hedged out in 1987
the risk of the market and the risk of the
sector.

257
00:29:33,320 --> 00:29:37,380
And for 25 years, you don't have that
risk.

258
00:29:38,100 --> 00:29:39,060
So what have you done?

259
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:49,980
You've paid the performance of Sainsbury,
100%, to obtain the performance of Tesco,
minus that, 1,300%,

260
00:29:49,980 --> 00:29:54,300
to hedge out all of those risks for that
whole amount of time.

261
00:29:56,140 --> 00:30:00,880
So throughout the length of that trade,
let's say you had that trade on for 25
years.

262
00:30:02,480 --> 00:30:05,380
Think of all the stuff that happened in
the market and happened in the world,

263
00:30:05,780 --> 00:30:10,820
where there were market shocks, where the
market dropped 10%, 20%, 30%.

264
00:30:10,820 --> 00:30:18,880
So 1992, the UK being kicked out of the
ERM.

265
00:30:20,600 --> 00:30:22,680
1987, the 87 crash.

266
00:30:25,560 --> 00:30:28,120
The tech bubble bursting, 2000.

267
00:30:29,720 --> 00:30:31,500
September the 11th, 2001.

268
00:30:34,560 --> 00:30:37,060
Asian, Russian debt crisis, 97, 98.

269
00:30:38,500 --> 00:30:40,960
Look at all these things that affect the
overall market.

270
00:30:41,700 --> 00:30:43,240
Now, what happens to your spread trade?

271
00:30:43,780 --> 00:30:49,620
What happens to your trade in that
situation when you're long one stock,
short another in the same sector?

272
00:30:50,340 --> 00:30:51,080
What happens?

273
00:30:52,880 --> 00:31:00,840
Well, if you're well hedged, and you've
hedged out the market risk properly, you
shouldn't expect to make or lose any money
in those situations.

274
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:04,980
Because they just both go down by the same
amount.

275
00:31:07,300 --> 00:31:09,940
So what are you actually doing in that
trade?

276
00:31:10,220 --> 00:31:12,580
You've hedged out the market risk and the
sector risk.

277
00:31:14,880 --> 00:31:18,060
What happens if there's a problem in the
food supply chain to the UK?

278
00:31:19,820 --> 00:31:21,200
Well, both stocks will be affected.

279
00:31:22,820 --> 00:31:24,280
So they both go down the same amount.

280
00:31:27,360 --> 00:31:37,140
What you're actually doing in that trade,
which is exactly the same trade as long
CMG, Chipotle Mexican grill, short
McDonald's.

281
00:31:39,720 --> 00:31:42,940
What you're actually doing is you're
isolating company risk.

282
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:47,300
So you're saying, I don't want to be
exposed to the market risk.

283
00:31:47,820 --> 00:31:49,820
I don't want to be exposed to the sector
risk.

284
00:31:49,820 --> 00:32:04,160
I want to isolate unique risk and be
exposed to the corporate plan of CMG or
Tesco relative to McDonald's or Sainsbury.

285
00:32:08,420 --> 00:32:13,860
So you're exposing yourself to the
management earnings plan of each company.

286
00:32:14,880 --> 00:32:16,520
And hedging out those two risks.

287
00:32:19,720 --> 00:32:23,140
So you get 1300% return, long Tesco, short
Sainsbury.

288
00:32:23,260 --> 00:32:24,200
And that's unleveraged.

289
00:32:24,940 --> 00:32:28,160
So imagine if you did that three or four
times leveraged.

290
00:32:29,940 --> 00:32:34,000
You've massively outperformed the stock
market and the sector over 25 years.

291
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:35,320
Massively.

292
00:32:40,320 --> 00:32:42,640
But for significantly less risk.

293
00:32:44,220 --> 00:32:49,520
Because all the guys who owned the market
in that time were exposed to the 87 crash.

294
00:32:50,620 --> 00:32:54,720
They were exposed to the UK being kicked
out of the ERM.

295
00:32:55,460 --> 00:32:59,620
They were exposed to the Russian-Asian
debt crisis of 97-98.

296
00:33:00,240 --> 00:33:02,420
They were exposed to the tech bubble
bursting.

297
00:33:05,020 --> 00:33:06,920
They were exposed to September the 11th.

298
00:33:06,980 --> 00:33:08,620
They were exposed to the subprime crisis.

299
00:33:10,060 --> 00:33:11,500
In your trade, you were not.

300
00:33:11,860 --> 00:33:12,760
Because they both fell.

301
00:33:14,380 --> 00:33:15,820
So you didn't make or lose any money.

302
00:33:16,560 --> 00:33:18,400
That's what we mean by hedging out the
risk.

303
00:33:19,580 --> 00:33:21,240
Now if you do it for shorter periods of
time,

304
00:33:21,940 --> 00:33:24,200
people get lazy because they think,

305
00:33:24,800 --> 00:33:27,140
I don't need to hedge it out because the
world's fine.

306
00:33:29,780 --> 00:33:31,600
I'm only going to have this trade on for
three months.

307
00:33:32,140 --> 00:33:33,080
But the world's fine.

308
00:33:33,780 --> 00:33:34,680
And then what happens?

309
00:33:34,880 --> 00:33:37,160
There's an earthquake in Japan and you
lose all your money.

310
00:33:39,200 --> 00:33:42,580
Now have a look at Chipotle Mexican Grill
and McDonald's.

311
00:33:43,740 --> 00:33:44,920
What's that trade saying?

312
00:33:45,140 --> 00:33:47,360
Well, you're hedging out market and sector
risk.

313
00:33:48,040 --> 00:33:49,480
But what does that trade actually say?

314
00:33:50,060 --> 00:33:51,560
Well, CMG's operating plan,

315
00:33:52,320 --> 00:33:53,500
they'll put it in front of you.

316
00:33:53,820 --> 00:33:55,820
They've got a fiduciary duty to the market

317
00:33:55,820 --> 00:33:58,640
to tell them what their operating plan is
for the next two, three, five years.

318
00:33:59,780 --> 00:34:04,940
Their operating plan two years ago, even
now,

319
00:34:05,480 --> 00:34:08,680
is to open a certain amount of stores in
Europe,

320
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:11,800
a certain amount of restaurants in Europe
and the US per month.

321
00:34:14,420 --> 00:34:16,260
So what do you think the stocks look like

322
00:34:16,260 --> 00:34:17,780
relative to each other in the last three
years?

323
00:34:22,880 --> 00:34:24,300
What do you think the stocks look like?

324
00:34:24,300 --> 00:34:24,300
What do you think the stocks look like?

325
00:34:29,780 --> 00:34:33,940
Let's just first go through the risk
profile of each trade.

326
00:34:34,640 --> 00:34:35,800
Cross-sector spread trade.

327
00:34:36,760 --> 00:34:38,100
You're hedging out market risk.

328
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:41,080
You're hedging out market risk.

329
00:34:41,500 --> 00:34:43,660
You've got sector risk and you've got
stock risk

330
00:34:43,660 --> 00:34:45,500
because you're exposed to all the stocks
in the sector.

331
00:34:47,260 --> 00:34:49,620
If one of those companies blows up,

332
00:34:50,340 --> 00:34:51,980
you're exposed to that

333
00:34:51,980 --> 00:34:53,720
because you own them in the sector.

334
00:34:54,680 --> 00:34:58,180
They might have a small percentage
contribution

335
00:34:58,180 --> 00:35:00,100
to the overall performance of the sector,

336
00:35:00,320 --> 00:35:01,580
but you've still got that risk.

337
00:35:05,240 --> 00:35:07,700
What about cross-sector constituent spread
trades?

338
00:35:09,460 --> 00:35:12,340
No market risk, sector risk and stock
risk.

339
00:35:13,760 --> 00:35:16,560
What about the two stocks in the same
sector?

340
00:35:20,300 --> 00:35:22,240
No market risk, no sector risk,

341
00:35:22,480 --> 00:35:23,600
but you've got stock risk.

342
00:35:24,320 --> 00:35:26,640
But you know that because you're isolating
that risk.

343
00:35:27,100 --> 00:35:28,060
When you take these trades,

344
00:35:28,540 --> 00:35:30,820
you take them with the knowledge

345
00:35:30,820 --> 00:35:33,540
that what risk you're isolating

346
00:35:33,540 --> 00:35:34,880
and what risk you're exposed to.

347
00:35:39,680 --> 00:35:41,280
So let's have a look at some of these
trades.

348
00:35:42,380 --> 00:35:45,500
Now what you do when you look at these
trades,

349
00:35:45,720 --> 00:35:48,600
they're called spread trades or ratio
trades.

350
00:35:50,900 --> 00:35:54,360
You take the share price or the price of
the asset

351
00:35:54,360 --> 00:35:55,820
that you're looking to go long

352
00:35:55,820 --> 00:35:59,620
and you put that on the top of the ratio.

353
00:35:59,980 --> 00:36:00,860
That's the numerator.

354
00:36:03,920 --> 00:36:06,800
That's the convention when you put these
trades together

355
00:36:06,800 --> 00:36:07,480
to look at them.

356
00:36:10,300 --> 00:36:14,480
So here we've got the S&P 500 consumer
discretionary sector.

357
00:36:15,680 --> 00:36:18,600
Short, S&P 500 utility sector.

358
00:36:19,600 --> 00:36:22,840
So the code for the consumer discretionary
sector in the US

359
00:36:22,840 --> 00:36:24,720
and you can trade it on ETF.

360
00:36:25,060 --> 00:36:26,540
Very, very liquid, very easy.

361
00:36:26,940 --> 00:36:28,100
You can be in and out in minutes.

362
00:36:30,180 --> 00:36:33,240
The code is XLY for consumer
discretionary.

363
00:36:34,240 --> 00:36:36,880
The code for the utility sector is XLU.

364
00:36:37,720 --> 00:36:40,560
And you just take the price of XLY

365
00:36:40,560 --> 00:36:42,040
and divide it by XLU.

366
00:36:42,560 --> 00:36:43,500
And you get a ratio.

367
00:36:48,820 --> 00:36:50,340
Ratio here is 1.36.

368
00:36:53,220 --> 00:36:56,340
So what do we think that looks like since
2009?

369
00:36:56,720 --> 00:36:57,780
Well, you've seen the performances.

370
00:37:00,620 --> 00:37:01,640
That's what it looks like.

371
00:37:03,080 --> 00:37:08,460
So it paid 76% since March 2009.

372
00:37:10,780 --> 00:37:12,480
So that's paid less than the market

373
00:37:12,480 --> 00:37:15,320
and less than the consumer discretionary
sector itself.

374
00:37:15,760 --> 00:37:15,860
Yeah?

375
00:37:17,680 --> 00:37:18,740
But what have you done?

376
00:37:20,740 --> 00:37:23,300
You've actually hedged out market risk.

377
00:37:24,360 --> 00:37:27,260
So you've paid money, the potential
return,

378
00:37:28,160 --> 00:37:29,680
to take less risk.

379
00:37:30,620 --> 00:37:31,800
To get that return.

380
00:37:34,080 --> 00:37:36,460
And what you'll find in the market,

381
00:37:37,540 --> 00:37:40,520
when you take, when you hedge out risk,

382
00:37:41,040 --> 00:37:42,800
your expected return is going to be lower.

383
00:37:46,160 --> 00:37:49,040
The home run trade is when you hedge out
risk

384
00:37:49,040 --> 00:37:51,620
and you actually get a better return

385
00:37:51,620 --> 00:37:53,320
than the market and the sector.

386
00:37:54,460 --> 00:37:55,720
That's the home run trade.

387
00:37:57,160 --> 00:37:58,940
And that's what you should always be
looking for.

388
00:37:59,180 --> 00:38:01,940
So that's an example of you paying more,

389
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:04,480
you paying to take out the risk.

390
00:38:11,360 --> 00:38:12,880
Cross-sector constituent.

391
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:16,700
So long CMG, short Entergy Corp.

392
00:38:17,640 --> 00:38:20,020
So longer consumer discretionary stock,

393
00:38:20,620 --> 00:38:22,740
short a US utility stock.

394
00:38:24,600 --> 00:38:25,880
What's the return there?

395
00:38:26,920 --> 00:38:29,240
415% since 2009.

396
00:38:29,780 --> 00:38:30,620
Non-leveraged.

397
00:38:33,900 --> 00:38:35,100
What does it look like?

398
00:38:38,460 --> 00:38:40,480
So you can see the ratio goes from 1

399
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:44,040
to 4.6 roughly.

400
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:47,060
That's the ratio going up.

401
00:38:48,940 --> 00:38:50,200
Because that's what you want

402
00:38:50,200 --> 00:38:51,580
when you look at these ratios.

403
00:38:51,840 --> 00:38:54,560
If you're long the asset on the top,

404
00:38:54,740 --> 00:38:55,200
the numerator,

405
00:38:55,960 --> 00:38:57,460
short the asset on the bottom,

406
00:38:57,800 --> 00:38:58,340
the denominator,

407
00:38:58,920 --> 00:39:00,020
what are you looking for?

408
00:39:00,360 --> 00:39:02,260
You're looking for the ratio to go up.

409
00:39:04,240 --> 00:39:04,460
Yeah?

410
00:39:07,740 --> 00:39:10,000
What about long CMG, short McDonald's?

411
00:39:13,580 --> 00:39:15,620
That's paid 194%

412
00:39:15,620 --> 00:39:17,260
over the same period.

413
00:39:18,560 --> 00:39:19,620
That's what it looks like.

414
00:39:25,160 --> 00:39:27,200
So what would you say

415
00:39:27,200 --> 00:39:28,200
is the favourite trade there?

416
00:39:31,520 --> 00:39:32,540
What's the favourite trade

417
00:39:32,540 --> 00:39:33,180
out of all of those?

418
00:39:41,600 --> 00:39:42,840
So what you've just seen there

419
00:39:42,840 --> 00:39:45,520
